# Leads opened from the July 2026 scoping review. These are works and corpora the
# review names without giving a citation precise enough to seed a source record,
# plus the corpus-enumeration and control-list gaps it exposes.
# Apply with `ibi ingest --record-type lead`.
{"id":"IBI-LEAD-WALLER-2025-PUBLICATION-LIST","lead_type":"citation","description":"Obtain Waller's 'The Aramaic Incantation Bowls: The State of the Art' (Marcus and Mokhtarian 2025, pp. 3-48) in full and enumerate its publication list of JBA bowl publications 1853-2024. This is the field's nearest available control list for what counts as published, and the only external check the index has on its own discovery saturation. The excerpt already archived covers printed pp. 10-13 only.","url":"https://judaicstudies.brown.edu/monograph/aramaic-incantation-bowls-their-late-antique-jewish-contexts","status":"open","priority":1,"resolution_notes":"Standing first acquisition priority in both the scoping review's Phase 1 and its July 2026 progress checkpoint. Interlibrary loan or a research library."}
{"id":"IBI-LEAD-GORDON-BOWL-EDITIONS","lead_type":"citation","description":"Enumerate Cyrus H. Gordon's bowl editions, published across many venues from 1934 to 1984, and record each as a separate source with its objects. The scoping review names them collectively without citations. Gordon is likely the largest single unrecorded publisher of bowls in the index: no Gordon record exists in `sources`.","status":"open","priority":1,"resolution_notes":"Backward-chain from Isbell 1975, which compiles then-published material, and forward-chain from Naveh and Shaked, who correct many Gordon readings."}
{"id":"IBI-LEAD-EARLY-DECIPHERMENTS","lead_type":"citation","description":"Establish precise citations for the 1853-1912 decipherment layer named by the scoping review §4.1: Thomas Ellis in Layard 1853, Moise Schwab, Joseph Halevy, Henry Hyvernat, Henri Pognon, Mark Lidzbarski, and J. A. Myhrman 1909. Only Schwab 1891 is currently in `sources`. Henry Hyvernat presently appears in the corpus only inside a `former_location` claim, not as a scholar.","status":"open","priority":2,"resolution_notes":"These publications carry the earliest object attributions and the least reliable findspots; they matter for META-005 evidence grading as much as for bibliography."}
{"id":"IBI-LEAD-MIDCENTURY-EDITIONS","lead_type":"citation","description":"Establish citations for the mid-century layer the scoping review §4.3 names without details: J. Obermann, W. H. Rossell's 1953 dissertation, Stephen A. Kaufman, and Tapani Harviainen's philological studies. None has a source record.","status":"open","priority":3}
{"id":"IBI-LEAD-MRLA8-TMH7-SOURCES","lead_type":"identifier","description":"Nineteen objects carry `MRLA 8::NN` designations and two carry `TMH 7::NN`, but neither publication has a source record. MRLA 8 is expected to be Ford and Morgenstern, Aramaic Incantation Bowls in Museum Collections vol. 1 (Hilprecht); TMH 7 is expected to be Muller-Kessler 2005. Confirm both identifications against the objects' reporting sources before creating the links, and do not assume the series numbers.","status":"open","priority":1,"resolution_notes":"Found by checking every `publication object key` prefix against `sources`. See data/reports/scoping_review_alignment_2026-09-05.md."}
{"id":"IBI-LEAD-VMBA-EJCM-CORPORA","lead_type":"other","description":"Record the two standing digital corpora as sources with a captured access date and data version, and reconcile their working counts against the index: the Virtual Magic Bowl Archive at Exeter (images and resources for roughly 650 Schoyen texts) and the European Jewish Classical Magic project at Ca' Foscari (approximately 500 JBA magical objects, about 125 Mandaic bowls, about 50 Syriac magical objects). Those are methodological working counts, not censuses.","status":"open","priority":2,"resolution_notes":"They are the only published external denominators against which the index's 1,320 identity hypotheses can be sanity-checked by language group."}
{"id":"IBI-LEAD-KHOUABIR-POGNON","lead_type":"other","description":"Enumerate the Khouabir Mandaic bowls published by Pognon 1898 and check whether any are present in the index under later museum or publication numbers. Khouabir is a named findspot cluster the index does not currently carry, and the review flags Pognon's classifications as requiring modern rechecking.","status":"open","priority":2}
{"id":"IBI-LEAD-B9002-SYRIAC-MANDAIC","lead_type":"identifier","description":"Locate Syriac bowl B9002, published by Gross and Scarlassara 2025 with multispectral imaging and a near-verbatim Mandaic incantation. Record it as an object-level instance of cross-script transmission, with the Mandaic parallel stored as a text relationship rather than as a language reclassification.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1163/17455227-bja10059","status":"open","priority":2}
{"id":"IBI-LEAD-DATED-BOWLS","lead_type":"other","description":"Assemble the absolutely dated bowls as a named cohort. The scoping review reports specimens assigned to 545, 573/574, 580, 585 and 611 CE, and Bhayro's chapter in Marcus and Mokhtarian 2025 treats dated objects specifically. These are the only anchors palaeographic chronology has, so they should be individually identified, graded, and kept separate from period attributions.","status":"open","priority":2,"resolution_notes":"Bears on META-002 and on the general problem that 513 dating claims are stored with no distinction between an object-level date and an inherited period."}
{"id":"IBI-LEAD-PAHLAVI-CORPUS","lead_type":"other","description":"Enumerate the Pahlavi and Middle Persian bowls. Two objects in the corpus carry a Middle Persian language claim; the scoping review treats Pahlavi as a distinct part of the material field that should not be folded into 'Aramaic bowls' without qualification. Ford and Morgenstern's Hilprecht volume includes Pahlavi and pseudo-script material and is the obvious starting point.","status":"open","priority":2}
